// examples/inventory/main.nika
//
// The same program `examples/report.nika` is, in **three files** - and the
// first thing in this repository that is more than one (Part I, 9.1).
//
// Input:  `category;item;count` per line
// Output: an HTML page written to the path given as the second argument
//
// Run:
//
//     nikaia build && ./main stock.csv report.html
//
// `crates/nikaia/tests/examples.rs` compiles and runs it, and checks the page.
//
// Why it is here beside `report.nika` rather than instead of it. `report.nika`
// is the same program in one file, and having both is the point: a reader can
// see what splitting a program buys and what it costs, on something small
// enough to hold in the head. What it buys here is that a reader of this file
// never has to open the parser, and that `weight` stays where it is used.
//
// **A package is a directory** (Part I, 9.1; ADR-047 D1), so there is no `use`
// between these three files and no prefix on the names they share: they are one
// namespace. Three things follow, and all three are the same answer - the
// lowering stays name for name (ADR-011 D2) because there is nothing to resolve.
//
//   * `total(…)` is written the way it is *because* that is what it lowers to.
//     One namespace here is one crate root there.
//   * **One ledger for the package** (Part III, 13.5), and one namespace in it -
//     so the type checker, the `sync` check and the provenance analysis work
//     across the files without learning a new trick.
//   * **`pub` says what leaves the package**, not what leaves the file. `weight`
//     is not public and this file may still call it; a *consumer* of this
//     package could not.

use std::fs
use std::cli

fn main() throws {
    let path = cli::args().nth(1) ?? "stock.csv"
    let out = cli::args().nth(2) ?? "report.html"

    let data = fs::map(&path) catch {
        eprintln(f"cannot open {path}: {error}")
        return
    }

    let mut entries = read(&data) catch {
        eprintln(f"{path}: {error}")
        return
    }

    entries.sort_by_key fn (entry) { -entry.count }

    let total = total(&entries)
    let count = entries.len()
    let markup = render(entries, total)

    fs::write(&out, &markup) catch {
        eprintln(f"cannot write {out}: {error}")
        return
    }

    println(f"{count} items, {total} in total")
}
